{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1468390-v5-homebrew-paths-of-enlightenment-mechanics","id":1468390,"title":"(V5 Homebrew) Paths of Enlightenment Mechanics","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-11-20T02:33:47-08:00","id":1468390,"title":"(V5 Homebrew) Paths of Enlightenment Mechanics","content":"While I personally don't intend to run a lot of Sabbat Campaigns with V5, Paths are still a nice thing to have for Antagonists so I thought I'd have something functional mechanically. This is a little bare bones, but the basic idea is that since Touchstones obviously doesn't quite jive with the Paths of Enlightenment, it's best if they're removed. In exchange though the Paths need some obvious downsides to avoid them just ending up more powerful than Humanity.<br /> <br /> This is what I came up with:<br /> <br /> Path following vampires lack the Humanity trait, instead having a trait known as \u201cBearing\u201d Determining how well they are to able to hold to their chosen path in exclusion to their original notions of consciousness and humanity. Though the most Jaded members of the Sabbat may boast otherwise, all vampires have some shred of conscious left, and even the paths are unable to fully dissolve these old ties to humanity. On its face, the two traits are similar. A Vampire with Bearing can still gain stains and degenerate when violating chronicle tenets, and they still have Convictions to aid them against this downward spiral, however the convictions granted by a Path of Enlightenment are oftentimes entirely inimical to any human forms of morality. Moreover, unlike the convictions of a Vampire with Humanity, the convictions of a Vampire with Bearing have no link to human touchstones.<br /> Vampires fully walking a path of enlightenment have no need for mortal touchstones, and indeed consider them a burden that proves the impossibility of Humanity for immortal creatures to shackle themselves to short-lived kine.<br /> <br /> While the lack of touchstones removes what most path-followers would consider a key vulnerability, the lack of human connections are their own weakness. While Bearing has the usual positive effects of giving bonuses to resist Frenzy, daysleep and determining length of Torpor as Humanity does, a High Bearing offers none of the benefits of more easily using the Blush of Life, mimicking humankind or similar benefits. Instead, each path gives a unique benefit for those who maintain a strong Bearing despite the difficulties. Finally, depending on how much you have given yourself over to a Path, you grow more and more obviously inhuman in your demeanor and outlook, regardless of your actual Bearing. Each conviction you adopt from your Path of Enlightenment gives you a cumulative one die penalty to interacting with mortals, capping at a three-die penalty for those who have fully adopted the path.<br /> <br /> Adopting a Path: Starting with a path is typically only appropriate for some characters with a significant history with the Sabbat or other organization endorsing such alternative forms of mortality. In such a case, the character starts with all three convictions of the path, as well as associated benefits and penalties for following one, however due to the difficulties in adopting a Path, such characters start with a Bearing of 5. It is far more common for characters, even most members of the Sabbat, to start with humanity and seek to adopt a Path in-play. Doing so is not a simple matter however, for the Path requires an entire reorientation of how a vampire interacts with the world. First, a Vampire must find someone who can mentor them in the path. This typically must be a trusted and well-versed Mawla, for Paths are difficult concepts to process, and not every adherent is suitable to teach others. Second, the initiate must fully divorce themselves from Humanity. Each time they do this, it requires an extended period of training as they actualize the teaching they have learned, usually only accomplished once per adventure. Doing this allows the Vampire to gain one of the three convictions associated with the Path they are adopting. This automatically inflicts 3 stains as they struggle to reconcile themselves with the monster that they are turning themselves into. If the Vampire already has three convictions, they must replace one of the ones that does not come from their current path with the new conviction. This is a process that is complicated by the existence of a touchstone. So long as the touchstone remains, the vampire cannot truly abandon it. While it is theoretically possible for a vampire to lose the touchstone through natural processes, most Path Mawlas insist upon the touchstone\u2019s destruction to assure adherence. Many of the most unforgiving Mawlas even demand that the initiate destroy their touchstones themselves, to ensure their loyalty to their new path.<br /> <br /> Example Paths-<br /> <br /> The Path of the Feral Heart<br /> Bonus: +1 Die bonus to cow and overwhelm others per Bearing above 7.<br /> Convictions:<ul><li>The Beast must Hunt</li> <li>The Pack is your life</li> <li>Cruelty is a pointless indulgence</li> </ul>The Path of Honorable Accord<br /> Bonus: +1 Die bonus to resist the temptations of others per Bearing above 7.<br /> Convictions:<ul><li>Your Word is your Oath</li> <li>Cowards are the lowest of creatures</li> <li>Your master\u2019s will is your own</li> </ul>The Path of the Power of the Inner Voice<br /> Bonus: +1 Die bonus to command others per Bearing above 7.<br /> Convictions:<ul><li>Personal Power is the Means to all Ends.</li> <li>Never Accept Failure</li> <li>Do not allow the unworthy to rule</li> </ul><br /> (Note: I've not seen the new Sabbat book so I don't know if any of this lines up with that information. Like I said, this is mostly a bare-bones attempt for a suitable mechanic. These path examples are based upon the information given in the V20 core rulebook.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24666-ravian","name":"Ravian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"46"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-20T06:57:42-08:00","id":1468398,"title":"","content":"Sounds okay, but lacks the personalization of &quot;Long Hard Road Out Of Hell&quot;'s beastmark system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-20T07:41:53-08:00","id":1468400,"title":"","content":"I like the design, certainly an interesting take and a nice stop-gap. If you mostly need these mechanics for NPC's or a short chronicle I think you could do a lot with them.<br /> <br /> In a longer-term game I'd consider:<br /> 1) Having no touchstones to protect is an objective advantage to Path Practitioners. What is there to counter-balance this choice? (I.E. why isn't every elder jumping onto a path.)<br /> <br /> 2) Whilst convictions certainly can convey path ethics, it does currently limit the perspectives of path follows to a singular understanding. Additionally, it means you can't (at least RAW if I remember rightly) gain stains for going against the strictures of your path. (An ST could of course award you stains if they felt appropriate, but RAW you only gain them for monstrous acts or violating chronicle tenets.)<br /> <br /> 3) What does the conversion to actualise, truly require? As per the V20 rules, there was no small degree of risk in transitioning to a path, a character could easily Wight whilst trying to convert. (You gain stains and need to resolve those of course, but you're risking losing only 3 humanity across all three conversions. That leaves most characters humanity 3/4 when they adopt a path.)<br /> <br /> Obviously, I say all the above having written one take on V5 Path rules for my Guide on the STV. No reason you have to address the above, just some considerations from my own design, but could be unneeded baggage! Paths are certainly very tricky to get right in V5's 'humanity-centric' design space.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STV Author - <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/374164/Guide-to-the-Sabbat?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=0_0_45624_0_0_0_0\" target=\"_blank\">Guide to the Sabbat</a> <br /> Current Project(s): Tales from the Fronts - A Guide to the Gehenna War","edited":{"userid":25061,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25061-karos","user":"Karos","date":".  Add clarity. 11-20-2021, 10:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25061-karos","name":"Karos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25061_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"91"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-20T11:06:08-08:00","id":1468414,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Karos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1468390-v5-homebrew-paths-of-enlightenment-mechanics?p=1468400#post1468400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the design, certainly an interesting take and a nice stop-gap. If you mostly need these mechanics for NPC's or a short chronicle I think you could do a lot with them.<br /> <br /> In a longer-term game I'd consider:<br /> 1) Having no touchstones to protect is an objective advantage to Path Practitioners. What is there to counter-balance this choice? (I.E. why isn't every elder jumping onto a path.)</div> </div> </div> </div>My intention was the flat penalties to interacting with humans while on a path were a significant downside. Characters on Paths simply hold such inhuman outlooks that they can't really blend in with humans effectively.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) Whilst convictions certainly can convey path ethics, it does currently limit the perspectives of path follows to a singular understanding. Additionally, it means you can't (at least RAW if I remember rightly) gain stains for going against the strictures of your path. (An ST could of course award you stains if they felt appropriate, but RAW you only gain them for monstrous acts or violating chronicle tenets.) </div> </div> </div>While it does say you only gain stains for violating convictions at your storyteller's discretion, I as a GM consider that very much appropriate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3) What does the conversion to actualise, truly require? As per the V20 rules, there was no small degree of risk in transitioning to a path, a character could easily Wight whilst trying to convert. (You gain stains and need to resolve those of course, but you're risking losing only 3 humanity across all three conversions. That leaves most characters humanity 3/4 when they adopt a path.) </div> </div> </div>I suppose it might be prudent to add in that a character has to be no more than Humanity 3 to begin adopting a Path, adding a clear element of risk as there's a non-zero chance that you end up a wight by the end of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Obviously, I say all the above having written one take on V5 Path rules for my Guide on the STV. No reason you have to address the above, just some considerations from my own design, but could be unneeded baggage! Paths are certainly very tricky to get right in V5's 'humanity-centric' design space. </div> </div> </div>No trouble, thanks for the suggestions, it did give some considerations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24666-ravian","name":"Ravian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"46"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-20T11:38:31-08:00","id":1468416,"title":"","content":"While you are supposed to take Touchstones due to the themes of V5,I'm quite sure there isn't <i>any actual penalty for not having any. </i><br /> <br /> Personally I'd do away with the concept with Touchstones for Paths all together and represent Path Ethics as Tenets. It works easily when going by V5's assumption that path's are per pack and not per Kindred, though a set of tenets for each one can work. This way any violation of a Path Ethic gives you stains, and you lack any conviction to protect you from it. <br /> <br /> You even could make Ritae wipe off some stains (or restore some willpower damage if there are none) to give a mechanical reason to participate in them, and give stains if they don't.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27145-setitefriend","name":"SetiteFriend","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"255"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-21T06:14:52-08:00","id":1468487,"title":"","content":"Humanity occupies a weird space in V5. I've recently finished watching the first season of LA by Night, and there's an awful lot of murder that, in V20, would've called for a Degeneration check in my games. But Convictions specifically allow you to rationalise away what you do. <br /> <br /> Pre-V5, I was always of the opinion that any rationalisation of your misdeeds should actually make it *harder* to avoid Degeneration, but that's entirely my opinion. Remorse is what keeps you on track. If you can justify something, it's easier to do again.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Touchstones make Humanity very vulnerable in an entirely different way and you automatically gain stains for certain behaviours that are monstrous rather than human. <br /> <br /> V5 Humanity is therefore, in my view, less about Morality and more about integrity and remaining connected to the human world. It's halfway between a sanity (or alienation) metre and a monstrosity guage.<br /> <br /> So, long story short, I think you may not need to make many changes to Humanity at all. What you have seems feasible and workable as is, because Humanity is more permissive in terms of morality. What you currently lack is the weakness that Touchstones represent. <br /> <br /> If you do want a drawback, I guess you could add in Beast Marks from The Long Road out of Hell, as those directly work with the new paradigm of what Humanity is. They make a nice substitute for Touchstones.<br /> <br /> Another idea is to steal the Beast Traits from MET V20. They add new triggers for Frenzy (e.g., Frustrated triggers a Range Frenzy check if you lose an opposed Mental test; Religion triggers a Terror Frenzy check if you oppose someone wielding a holy symbol or if you touch one). They're a bit simpler and not as flavourful, but they definitely work as drawbacks.<br /> <br /> What makes Paths inherently harder to replicate in V5, again IMO, is that it's much harder to work your way up the ladder than in V20. Paths were in some ways an inverted Humanity track, because you start low and work your way up. And I'm not sure what the best way to replicate that is in the current system.<br /> <br /> I've toyed with an inverse stains system, where positive actions that engage with and reinforce the ethics of your Path can reward you with &quot;enlightenment&quot; points (that name is a placeholder). If you can get 10 of these points, before stains start to eat into them, you can trade them in for a dot in your Path.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-21T06:39:00-08:00","id":1468489,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Karos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1468390-v5-homebrew-paths-of-enlightenment-mechanics?p=1468400#post1468400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Paths are certainly very tricky to get right in V5's 'humanity-centric' design space.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it's as tricky as it seems at first. They just choose to not make it as obvious in the text as they could have made.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1468390-v5-homebrew-paths-of-enlightenment-mechanics?p=1468414#post1468414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My intention was the flat penalties to interacting with humans while on a path were a significant downside.</div> </div> </div> </div>From a design standpoint it doesn't work so well. It could be as significant as the Touchstones themselves if the character had any reason to even try, but most of the time they won't. In fact, the only thing you achieve by making this interaction ever harder is to convince players to not try.<br /> <br /> Think always about how the rule nudges player behavior. Touchstones make the PCs seek human interactions, they are a mechanical incentive for a player to invest time in exploring how the character interacts with mortals and for the ST to pull those personal aspects of the PCs into the chronicle. So they add a touch of nuance to the characters and avenues for drama to the chronicle, all the while empowering players to color those things as they see fit.<br /> <br /> What does this penalty adds to the character or the play experience?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1468390-v5-homebrew-paths-of-enlightenment-mechanics?p=1468414#post1468414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While it does say you only gain stains for violating convictions at your storyteller's discretion, I as a GM consider that very much appropriate.</div> </div> </div> </div>You may then choose to incorporate this directly to your rules, but in general when designing rules it is advisable to distinguish your personal take from the advise you think is broadly applicable. They made stains for Convictions into an option instead of a rule for a reason.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1468390-v5-homebrew-paths-of-enlightenment-mechanics?p=1468414#post1468414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I suppose it might be prudent to add in that a character has to be no more than Humanity 3 to begin adopting a Path, adding a clear element of risk as there's a non-zero chance that you end up a wight by the end of it.</div> </div> </div> </div>It seems fair.<br /> <br /> ===========================================<br /> Well, I have my own suggestions on what to do, in part because I think the simplest solution is usually the better, and this one seems like too convoluted for me.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>1 - The Diet Path:</b><br /> <br /> Your Path is your personal list of Convictions. They may be attached to mortal Touchstones as normal, those may be people you still value and don't want to risk wassail for them, people you despise and want to follow a Path to divorce yourself from them or seek to harm them, or the normal gist, people that still manage to represent that Conviction for you (not all Convictions are Inhumane, not all humans are Humane).<br /> <br /> Alternatively, you can choose a non-human Touchstone. An object, place, another vampire. The ST must approve the choice, and such Touchstones may require more involved and complicated interaction than seems at first. Objects and places require protection and maintenance, and as they are Touchstones you have to be involved on it yourself. Vampires are likely to find out and explore the fact.<br /> <br /> <i>When to use:</i> The Diet Path should work well on Chronicles where Tenets have been chosen carefully and have to be distinct of one's Path. A Path follower living among Humanity followers, for example, would still suffer the social pressure that results in Stains against Tenets. Even a Sabbat game where the Pack is meant to follow a given Path defined in the Tenets may have a member or two that follow another Path through a Diet Path.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>2 - The Hardcore Path:</b><br /> <br /> Use Chronicle Tenets. At the ST discretion it may be either that everyone is on the same Path or that each character follow their own Chronicle Tenets. Your Convictions still work as normal and have normal Touchstones, after all they're your personal reasons to not fall from grace when going against your Path.<br /> <br /> <i>When to use</i><i>:</i> This is probably the best default for Path use. The rule's right there in the core and there's no reason to not use it, at most tweak it for a different set of Tenets for each character. Any game where Path followers are common and no standard must be defined is good with it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>3 - The Meritorious Path:</b><br /> <br /> Add a new Merit to the roster, Path Follower. Each dot adds an exclusive Tenet for that character only, derived from the chosen Path. In exchange the character ignores 1 stain per dot incurred by other Tenets each chapter and may also use the Path Tenets as Convictions at the ST's discretion.<br /> <br /> With 5 dots, or less if the ST feels like it, the character has a full conversion and don't follow Chronicle Tenets anymore, but the ST may get tougher on Stains incurred by Path Tenets.<br /> <br /> <i>When to use:</i> this is good when the ST and group are ok with Paths in the game, but the ST wants Path followers to have a literal price for the benefit of ignoring Chronicle Tenets. This is particularly interesting in chronicles of espionage, warfare and others where the Tenets are meant to be creatively broken instead of actually followed/broken for drama, or chronicles where a number of NPCs may react favorably to finding a dedicated Path Follower. This is <b>not</b> an adequate representation of the initiation into a Path unless you want to make it more mechanically than dramatically taxing.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>4 - The Conversion Process:</b><br /> <br /> Changing from Humanity to a Path is a dramatic shift and risky for Kindred. This is not about understanding and believing in a new morality, but instead internalizing this morality enough to steel your psyche against the Beast. In this sense, understanding and believing in the Path is just the beginning of the journey.<br /> <br /> First you must acquire your Path as Convictions, which can be done in any of three ways. The hard way is to act in service of the desired Conviction to the point of incurring Degeneration at least once in the session. If you can do this without acting against the desired Conviction or any Path Conviction not even once for this entire session, you may take the Conviction.<br /> <br /> You may sacrifice your normal Convictions for the medium or the easy way. The medium is to incur stains (not necessarily Degeneration) specifically by acting against your old Conviction in service of the new. Change the two of them and keep the Touchstone attached to the new, as you choose to let them live. This will let you recover the old Conviction later. The easy way is to sacrifice the very Touchstone to the new Conviction. Straightforward and instant reward, but you'll be one Conviction short in the future.<br /> <br /> Once you have three Path Convictions you may start the process of turning them into your personal Tenets. You do so by violating a tenet in service of the target Conviction in such a way that you either incur Degeneration or 3 stains at once. Burn a Tenet of your choice and replace with the new Tenet. Rinse and repeat until you're satisfied, it's advisable that you burn first the Tenets more similar to your Path, or you may end up with something really hard to violate royally and follow your Path at the same time. If a chronicle have more than 3 Tenets, replace the rest by more Path Tenets after the third time.<br /> <br /> At any time during this process in which you have a Conviction without a Touchstone, that Conviction don't have mechanical benefits, but counts as a Tenet to be followed during this process. After turning a Path Conviction into a Path Tenet, if you still have a Touchstone for that Conviction, you can recover your old Conviction, it works as normal.<br /> <br /> <i>When to use:</i> the Conversion Process is meant to explore the personal investment and danger of changing from Humanity to a Path. Sometimes it is better to just make the transition happen in downtime or as character background, but for the gritty, risky and dramatic representation of how much one must sink before raising as a Path Follower, those rules make for a journey of damnation and rebirth. Just be aware that it can also make the character loose up to <b>six</b> dots of Humanity easy before the end.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}